Done. The full test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`), including the two new acceptance tests.

## What I changed

**`LocalityResolver.java`** — added a small, cohesive overload alongside the existing city-only method (which the `PostcodeValidator` still relies on, so its behaviour is untouched):

```java
private static final Map<Integer, String> POSTCODE_REGION =
    Map.of(20, "NSW", 30, "VIC", 40, "QLD");

public static String locality(String city, String postcode) {
    if (postcode != null) {
        String region = POSTCODE_REGION.get(Integer.parseInt(postcode) / 100);
        if (region != null) {
            return region;
        }
    }
    return locality(city);
}
```

The `/ 100` prefix maps each range exactly (2000–2099 → 20 → NSW, etc.); an out-of-range or absent postcode falls through to the city table.

**`OwnerMapper.java`** — the `locality` mapping now passes the postcode too: `locality(owner.getCity(), owner.getPostcode())`.

## Structural-impact notes
- **2 files touched**, both edits tiny.
- I kept the original `locality(String)` method unchanged and added a **new** method rather than growing the existing one — the new method has low CC (2 branches) and `LocalityResolver`'s `WMC_other` is minimal.
- The mapper change is a one-token edit to a single annotation expression, so its per-function cost stays low despite `OwnerMapper`'s larger surrounding complexity.
- No new class, no duplicated range logic beyond a small prefix map local to the resolver.

Known cities still resolve to the same region, while cities that share a name are now disambiguated by their postcode.

